WEBVTT

00:00.600 --> 00:04.600
Hello everyone, I'm going to present a dynamic geometry software

00:04.600 --> 00:08.400
that I might have called YAGS, because it is not the first one, far from it,

00:08.400 --> 00:11.700
and I'm guilty myself of having already written two of them.

00:12.000 --> 00:16.800
One is CaRMetal, in 2006, built on the engine of C.a.R.,

00:16.800 --> 00:19.600
a software developed by René Grothmann,

00:19.600 --> 00:23.200
and the other is DGPad, in 2012, this time starting from a blank page,

00:23.200 --> 00:27.600
mainly to adapt dynamic geometry as well as possible to mobile devices.

00:27.600 --> 00:33.900
So the new software I'm going to talk about is not called YAGS, it's called DocGeo,

00:34.300 --> 00:40.300
and I'm weak enough to think it is not just one more software in this field.

00:41.000 --> 00:46.400
But let's get back to the subject of this video: the presentation of DocGeo,

00:46.600 --> 00:52.700
in which we will see four very different ways of making geometric constructions.

00:52.700 --> 00:58.700
The first way to build is the DGPad way, in a mode that could be called prefix,

00:58.900 --> 01:00.600
where the object comes first.

01:00.800 --> 01:05.000
As you saw, contextual tools appear in the ring,

01:05.200 --> 01:07.400
depending on the object you click.

01:07.900 --> 01:11.700
For example, here, if I click the line, I only get a few tools,

01:11.900 --> 01:15.500
among them a perpendicular, which I apply to point D.

01:15.500 --> 01:21.900
And for this intersection point E, for example, the ring will contain a locus icon,

01:22.100 --> 01:24.800
because it depends on a point D that moves along the line.

01:25.200 --> 01:33.400
I rename point C, and of course I get the parabola defined by focus and directrix.

01:33.400 --> 01:42.200
I take advantage of this finished figure to point out that DocGeo handles sets of figures very well.

01:42.500 --> 01:46.500
From DocGeo, you can save workbooks of figures,

01:46.600 --> 01:50.100
exactly like the ones found in today's spreadsheets.

01:50.700 --> 01:54.500
This makes it possible to save several figures on the same topic

01:54.600 --> 01:56.200
in one single file.

01:57.300 --> 02:00.000
Very quickly now, another way to build,

02:00.000 --> 02:05.600
but you all know it: it's the way of every other dynamic geometry software,

02:05.700 --> 02:06.500
as far as I know.

02:06.700 --> 02:12.100
You show the tool first, and then the object the tool will apply to.

02:12.300 --> 02:15.700
Right now, I'm building a perpendicular bisector,

02:16.200 --> 02:19.400
I just change the name of point B,

02:19.700 --> 02:23.600
and then I'll apply the locus tool to the line, since that's possible,

02:24.300 --> 02:26.800
and get the envelope,

02:26.800 --> 02:33.300
and so the ellipse and the hyperbola defined by director circle and focus.

02:33.300 --> 02:39.700
I take advantage of this figure to point out that DocGeo is a true 3D software,

02:39.900 --> 02:41.900
designed around 3D from the start,

02:42.500 --> 02:46.500
and that the 2D part we have seen so far is, as you can see here,

02:47.000 --> 02:49.800
only a restriction to the plane z = 0.

02:50.200 --> 02:54.000
So switching to 3D mode by clicking the cube-shaped icon

02:54.000 --> 02:58.200
made icons appear in the tool palette that are not there in 2D,

02:58.200 --> 03:03.700
but we'll see that a little later in this video.

03:05.600 --> 03:08.200
Here is a third way to build,

03:08.300 --> 03:11.100
in a mode that could be called multi-prefix,

03:11.200 --> 03:14.600
which consists in first selecting several objects,

03:15.100 --> 03:19.900
and then the palette, with some icons grayed out and others not, offers the possible tools.

03:19.900 --> 03:25.700
I choose the Polygon tool, then BC and A to get the altitude,

03:26.100 --> 03:31.300
the same for AB and C, and finally B and AC.

03:33.600 --> 03:37.200
All that remains is to create the orthocenter the same way,

03:37.500 --> 03:39.800
with the Intersection tool.

03:41.200 --> 03:44.900
Fourth and last way to build in DocGeo, well known to GeoGebra users,

03:45.600 --> 03:48.300
which consists in typing commands in the construction panel.

03:48.300 --> 03:51.300
Here, typing f(x) = a x² + b x + c

03:51.300 --> 03:54.400
automatically created three sliders in the construction.

03:55.100 --> 03:56.300
I show the axes,

03:56.700 --> 04:02.200
and by right-clicking the curve, I change its style, for example the thickness

04:03.000 --> 04:05.200
and the opacity of the line.

04:06.500 --> 04:11.100
With a right-click on an empty area of the figure,

04:11.600 --> 04:16.300
I can change a whole lot of settings related to the look of the axes

04:16.300 --> 04:20.200
and of the grid, which is what I'm doing now.

04:22.400 --> 04:24.900
For example, positive half-axis.

04:26.100 --> 04:28.100
I go on with my construction

04:28.100 --> 04:34.200
by asking for a point on the graph of the function,

04:34.200 --> 04:38.900
with x-coordinate 3.

04:38.900 --> 04:44.200
Then I'll ask for the tangent, for example, the tangent at point A.

04:47.200 --> 04:51.400
And I'll finish by pointing out that every typed command

04:51.400 --> 04:56.000
comes with automatic completion suggestions for the command name,

04:56.400 --> 04:58.200
as well as for its arguments.

04:58.200 --> 05:05.700
Well, episode 2 now: how to build in 3D.

05:06.400 --> 05:09.500
With a really very simple example, I'll go very fast.

05:09.800 --> 05:16.500
On the floor of the 3D view, I place a cube, standing on the floor.

05:17.400 --> 05:23.000
Then I place three points on three different faces of this cube.

05:23.000 --> 05:29.900
I ask for the plane CDE, by pointing at these three points in turn.

05:30.700 --> 05:36.500
And then I simply ask for the section of this cube by the plane.

05:39.300 --> 05:40.700
There, I'm done.

05:40.900 --> 05:43.600
Now I'll move the points to see that everything works well.

05:44.600 --> 05:48.100
You can also see the dashed lines for hidden edges.

05:48.600 --> 05:52.000
I removed the plane, and I'll also change the opacity of the cube

05:52.000 --> 05:56.900
to give a little more weight to the section itself

05:56.900 --> 05:58.800
that you see before you.

05:59.000 --> 06:03.000
Of course, by moving the points on each of these faces,

06:03.300 --> 06:06.400
I see the section change.

06:08.800 --> 06:14.100
In this episode, we'll see that DocGeo still bears the legacy of DGPad,

06:14.100 --> 06:16.900
since it also lets you program with blocks,

06:16.900 --> 06:21.000
but with an approach even closer to that of Scratch.

06:21.000 --> 06:26.000
So here is an example of programming with the DocGeo turtle.

06:27.200 --> 06:31.300
I start by creating a slider a, with numbers between 3 and 500,

06:31.300 --> 06:32.800
and these numbers will be integers.

06:33.100 --> 06:34.900
So I set the step to 1.

06:35.600 --> 06:39.300
With the turtle tool, I create a point A on the fly

06:39.300 --> 06:43.700
on which I'll program a turtle trace that draws a rosette

06:43.700 --> 06:46.200
made by rotating a square around A.

06:46.200 --> 06:48.000
So I start by building the square.

06:48.100 --> 06:54.000
I choose "units" so that it follows the zoom, and I finish with the turn.

06:55.200 --> 06:57.700
Then I take care of rotating this square

06:57.700 --> 07:01.500
using the value of slider a.

07:02.200 --> 07:04.500
And then I take care of the rotation angle:

07:04.500 --> 07:13.800
360 divided by that same expression a.

07:16.100 --> 07:19.400
Moving the slider shows you a fundamental point.

07:19.600 --> 07:23.400
What you see, built by the turtle, is not just a drawing,

07:23.400 --> 07:27.800
but a true geometric object, just like a line, a circle, a curve or a locus.

07:28.500 --> 07:34.200
That's why, from its implementation in DGPad, we spoke of a dynamic turtle.

07:35.200 --> 07:39.100
And since the turtle is just as comfortable in 3D, I'll show you the equivalent,

07:39.100 --> 07:41.000
which turns the rosette into a book.

07:41.300 --> 07:47.000
The only difference is using a "tilt" block instead of "turn".

07:48.400 --> 07:52.700
Now, episode 4: or how a point can be magnetized,

07:52.700 --> 07:56.600
attracted by other objects, starting with a very simple figure

07:56.600 --> 08:02.800
whose construction steps I'll let you watch in fast motion.

08:03.800 --> 08:07.100
It mainly consists of three semicircles and a perpendicular bisector.

08:07.900 --> 08:10.800
In this kind of situation, a fundamental question

08:10.800 --> 08:13.700
I asked myself in 2006 while programming CaRMetal:

08:13.700 --> 08:18.900
wouldn't it be wonderful, in this figure, if point H were attracted by the bisector,

08:18.900 --> 08:23.500
the two intersection points and the semicircle with diameter AB?

08:23.800 --> 08:27.300
And that's where the magnet feature comes in.

08:27.900 --> 08:34.800
For the bisector, for example, I set the attraction slider to 15 pixels.

08:34.800 --> 08:42.500
The same for the two intersection points, and finally for the semicircle.

08:42.600 --> 08:48.500
After that, the point is attracted as soon as it is less than 15 pixels from one of these four objects.

08:48.500 --> 08:55.400
Here the first intersection point, the second one, and then the semicircle with diameter AB.

08:55.400 --> 09:01.200
To end episode 4, I'll let you see what happens

09:01.200 --> 09:05.100
when the attraction is set to "everywhere", that is, infinity.

09:05.800 --> 09:08.700
This way, a point can be defined on several objects.

09:08.900 --> 09:14.100
Here, for example, point M is attracted everywhere by the circle and the five other segments.

09:15.000 --> 09:17.200
And so ends season 1.

09:17.700 --> 09:18.400
Wait a minute…

09:18.600 --> 09:20.900
Yes, I forgot to tell you about a small detail

09:20.900 --> 09:24.200
about importing files from other software.

09:24.200 --> 09:29.500
No one will be surprised that DocGeo can open DGPad figures, for historical reasons.

09:30.000 --> 09:31.800
But what may be more surprising

09:31.800 --> 09:35.200
is that DocGeo also opens a good number of GeoGebra files.

09:35.800 --> 09:39.200
I'll show you a few GGB files that I downloaded

09:39.200 --> 09:42.800
from the official GeoGebra website and opened as is in both programs.

09:42.900 --> 09:44.500
I promise, there's no trickery.

09:44.900 --> 09:49.300
At the top, the figure opened in DocGeo, and at the bottom in GeoGebra.

09:49.700 --> 09:52.400
Since this is, for now, a very simple figure,

09:52.900 --> 09:56.500
it's easy to see that the import respects the figure.

09:57.500 --> 10:01.300
Here, a perhaps slightly more complex figure, with winding ellipses.

10:01.500 --> 10:04.600
The author also used animations.

10:05.300 --> 10:08.900
This one, which I particularly like, because the author took care

10:08.900 --> 10:12.600
to lay the figure over an image of the Mandelbrot set.

10:13.200 --> 10:18.700
This gives a rather phenomenal exploration, I think.

10:19.400 --> 10:26.500
You'll also notice how extremely smooth DocGeo is while point C is being dragged.

10:26.600 --> 10:27.500
It's very, very fast.

10:27.600 --> 10:30.400
Anyway, 200 segments don't scare DocGeo, far from it.

10:31.300 --> 10:37.600
Then there's 3D, of course, with its cube nets.

10:37.900 --> 10:43.700
DGPad did this very well too; here it's done on a GeoGebra file.

10:43.700 --> 10:51.500
Another 3D file, with the three pyramids that make up the cube,

10:52.500 --> 10:55.300
opened in both programs, once again.

10:56.100 --> 10:58.700
And that's the real end of season 1.

10:59.100 --> 11:02.400
In season 2, we'll see, among other things,

11:03.100 --> 11:07.400
a short episode on the DocGeo desktop application,

11:07.500 --> 11:12.800
then macro-constructions, which I haven't mentioned at all in this season 1,

11:13.600 --> 11:16.900
animations, and AI in all this:

11:17.500 --> 11:23.000
a question that concerns the development of DocGeo.

11:24.000 --> 11:25.700
Thank you.
